Figure 4 illustrates the active transport and uptake of Nano-11 nanoparticles by antigen-presenting cells (APCs) in the draining lymph node over time, supporting its role as a vaccine adjuvant. Flow cytometry plots and a quantitative graph demonstrate that Nano-11-containing myeloid cells, predominantly migratory dendritic cells (MigDCs), accumulate in the lymph node, peaking around day 2 post-injection, and then decline. MigDCs consistently show the highest percentage of Nano-11 and the highest mean fluorescence intensity, indicating efficient uptake by these immunopotentiating cells. This active targeting to MigDCs is essential for initiating an effective adaptive immune response.